Capture the Windows error before the target is destroyed in SpawnTarget.

FinishSpawnTargetImpl called GetLastError after ApplyToTarget had returned, but
on failure ApplyToTarget had already destroyed the target, which terminates the
process and could overwrite the last error.

Make ApplyToTarget only take ownership of the target on success, so that the
last error is read while the target is still alive. Also read it when
DropActiveProcessLimit fails, before the target is terminated.

The last error is cleared before each call, so that failures that are not
caused by a Windows API call are unlikely to report a stale error.

diff --git a/sandbox/win/src/broker_services.cc b/sandbox/win/src/broker_services.cc
index e40fffbad0fc..ac36c847b324 100644
--- a/sandbox/win/src/broker_services.cc
+++ b/sandbox/win/src/broker_services.cc
@@ -612,15 +612,18 @@ ResultCode BrokerServicesBase::FinishSpawnTargetImpl(
     // Restrict the job from containing any processes. Job restrictions
     // are only applied at process creation, so the target process is
     // unaffected.
+    ::SetLastError(ERROR_SUCCESS);
     ResultCode result = policy_base->DropActiveProcessLimit();
     if (result != SBOX_ALL_OK) {
+      *last_error = ::GetLastError();
       target->Terminate();
       return result;
     }
   }
 
-  // Now the policy is the owner of the target. TargetProcess will terminate
-  // the process if it has not completed when it is destroyed.
+  // On success the policy becomes the owner of the target. TargetProcess will
+  // terminate the process if it has not completed when it is destroyed.
+  ::SetLastError(ERROR_SUCCESS);
   ResultCode result = policy_base->ApplyToTarget(std::move(target));
 
   if (result != SBOX_ALL_OK) {
